Explanation of the code, and the general flow of the code:

1) The first section is for macro statements, for defining variable names to directly write to the registers without using any dereferencing operations in the main code. This entire code is written around timer0 module.


2) Now, we will be using CTC mode, and for this code, CTC mode is used because it is more useful to calculate times with less residuals. 

I used the OCR0A register, while using the timer0 module 
This code only uses a polling mechanism / busy wait loop for reading the OCF0A flag, once it is detected to be set, we make it zero by writing a logical one , as specified by the datasheet.

3) A prescaler of 1024 is used, set by setting the CS02:00 bits to 1-0-1, since the master clock for the Arduino UNO board, is the crystal oscillator on chip, generating a 16mhz signal, each incremental tick going to the tcnt0 is for 64  microseconds.

The exact math: 

Prescaler = 1024
master clock frequency = 16mhz
ocr0a value is set to 250, for this code. so for every one ocf0a, 16 milliseconds(64 microseconds * 250) have passed.

So, if we divide 1000 ms(1s = 1000ms) by 16 milliseconds, we get 62.5,which means we have to read the OCF0A flag 62 times. 

I am not dealing with the slight error im getting, because if i only count 62 I'll be observing a delay of 992 ms, and if I do 63 reads, i'll be seeing 1008ms.


4) The WGM bits are set first, the tcnt0 register is cleared, and the ocr0a register is set to 250, variables status and count are initialized, and the entire rest of the function, sleepself() is just polling the ocf0a flag and then writing a logical one to it to clear it and incrementing count variable, this process only ends once the count variable reached 63. (less than or equal to condition) which means the loop runs for 63 times, making the delay for over 1 second. Precisely speaking 1008ms. (ms = milliseconds)




