1) I Counted the number of ticks for just ONE overflow and didn't poll TOV0 just counted the ticks for ONE overflow cycle, I only counted 250 ticks, I did this because I counted each incremental tick to be 16 microseconds and went till 250 for a 4 second delay. It's stupid, very stupid, in hindsight, but I've recognized the mistakes in my math, and fixed it. Here i mistook 16 microseconds to be 16 milliseconds and thought the register running from 0->250 will be a 4 second delay. My entire understanding of the math at the beginning point of time was very skewed to say the least. 

2) A mistake I've done is Setting the CS bits to 1 1 1(binary), which read me the value for tcnt0 as 0, because the timer0 module was being clocked by an external clock source, but there was no external source in my case, so the timer0/tcnt0 never got incremented. 

3) For a way to understand even if the timer was being incremented or not, and i saw the values going up very fast which, after sometime, made me realize, the values sometimes miss, this was when I was polling tcnt0 if itwas above 250, the code was to stop the loop.

This had a MAJOR problem, as software limitations start to kick in, you see, even if u were checking if the value of tcnt0 was above 250, the actual operation was done on variables inside the program, not hardware enforced. This means, sometimes you'd miss the values, as the timer is incrementing way too fast.

That is why, using CTC mode for cases like this, is way more reliable, as the comparison is done for each cycle, and it is ensured that there is "slipping". 

